From: slaren <slarengh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: let the frame generator stop before gating the clock

swrm_runtime_suspend() sends ClockStopNow and gates hclk straight away,
but the frame generator does not stop at once: on the Surface Laptop 7
(x1e80100, two WSA884x amplifiers on the WSA bus) its status bit was
still set in 17 to 45 % of the suspends and took 82 to 315 us to clear.
Gating the clock while the bus still runs appears to leave the
amplifiers unable to resync at the next clock-stop exit: one or both
fall off the bus and the speakers stay silent until the controller is
reset.

Wait for the frame generator to stop, polling the frame generator status
that swrm_wait_for_frame_gen_enabled() waits on at resume, for up to
30 ms, before the clock is gated, and warn if it does not stop.

On the laptop (7.3.1 + 0001 + 0002 + this patch): 400 and 300 idle/wake
cycles without a lost amplifier and without bus error messages, first
sound after an idle bus 50 ms (at most 90 ms); system suspend/resume
fine. Without it, 1 to 6 of 100 wakes lost an amplifier.

Backport to linux-surface 7.3.1 of the mainline draft, see
surface-laptop-7/mainline/0002-*.patch (based on soundwire fixes).
Replaces the earlier force_no_clk_stop_devs workaround, which gave up
clock stop on the WSA bus (about 250 ms more until the first sound).

Fixes: 74e79da9fd46 ("soundwire: qcom: add runtime pm support")
---
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/completion.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
@@ -134,6 +135,8 @@
 
 #define MAX_FREQ_NUM						1
 #define TIMEOUT_MS						100
+/* Time the frame generator gets to stop after ClockStopNow, in us */
+#define SWRM_FRM_GEN_STOP_TIMEOUT_US			30000
 #define QCOM_SWRM_MAX_RD_LEN					0x1
 #define DEFAULT_CLK_FREQ					9600000
 #define SWR_INVALID_PARAM					0xFF
@@ -1735,6 +1738,25 @@
 	clk_disable_unprepare(ctrl->hclk);
 }
 
+static u32 swrm_frame_gen_status(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl)
+{
+	u32 comp_sts = 0;
+
+	ctrl->reg_read(ctrl, ctrl->reg_layout[SWRM_REG_FRAME_GEN_ENABLED],
+		       &comp_sts);
+
+	return comp_sts;
+}
+
+static bool swrm_wait_for_frame_gen_disabled(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl)
+{
+	u32 comp_sts;
+
+	return !read_poll_timeout(swrm_frame_gen_status, comp_sts,
+				  !(comp_sts & SWRM_FRM_GEN_ENABLED), 100,
+				  SWRM_FRM_GEN_STOP_TIMEOUT_US, false, ctrl);
+}
+
 static int __maybe_unused swrm_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
@@ -1828,6 +1850,14 @@
 			dev_err(dev, "bus clock stop failed %d", ret);
 			return ret;
 		}
+
+		/*
+		 * ClockStopNow has been sent, but the frame generator needs a
+		 * moment to stop. Gating hclk before it has stopped can leave
+		 * the Peripherals out of sync at the next clock stop exit.
+		 */
+		if (!swrm_wait_for_frame_gen_disabled(ctrl))
+			dev_warn(dev, "frame generator did not stop before the clock was gated\n");
 	}
 
 	disable_irq(ctrl->irq);
